					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/dj-suptic" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(562665); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=562665&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1607266793" alt="DJ Suptic" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=562665)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/dj-suptic" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(562665); return false;" >DJ Suptic</a></span> <span class="odate time_1420837768 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">09 Jan 2015 21:09</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-2201947">
									

<blockquote>
<p>SCP-2860 is to be contained within a Type S Standard Humanoid Anomaly Containment Cell at Site-19.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Right off the start, I love it. No needing to pester the reader with exacting details, or how long the bed is, or the GPF of the toilet. Just a quick, simple setup, informative enough to know what's being held and the accommodations we have for the item, but leaving enough to the reader's imagination so they paint their own picture of a Standard Humanoid Anomaly Containment Cell. It provides us the palette, paint, and brush, to let us design and visualize our personal version of this particular part of the SCP Foundation.</p>
<p>I feel this should be done more in general, for items that don't require an exactly defined containment location for adequate containment. Too often I see all these unnecessarily precise measurements and, often asinine detail put into containment procedures. The idea of the Standard Containment Cell (for humanoids, or documents, or electronic equipment, or whatever) helps keep clutter out of articles, and helps draw the reader in, since they're populating their <em>own</em> mental version of the SCP Foundation, instead of a strictly dictated version of the writer.</p>
<p>Anyways, this is getting weirdly off track. The SCP is great - I love the trope of Researcher Becoming Subject, and this is an interesting story written into a good SCP article</p>
<p>+1</p>

							</div>

								
					
