					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/harakuroi" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1411063); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=1411063&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1607266793" alt="Harakuroi" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=1411063)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/harakuroi" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1411063); return false;" >Harakuroi</a></span> <span class="odate time_1441855802 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">10 Sep 2015 03:30</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-2372872">
									

<p>It's not very fair to say that the Foundation never learns anything from SCPs. Each article is only a snapshot in time, so of course they're going to contain a lot of works in process. But we have a several Neutralised articles containing things that are considered basic science now, but were once perceived as supernatural. I consider that evidence that the Foundation does sometimes come to completely understand an SCP, and then that knowledge simply because science. For those whose headcanon suggests that the Foundation has more advanced science that we know, the reason they have access to such advanced technology is because of their analysis and limited understanding of various SCPs. Even basic things like meme-kill agents or amnestics were probably developed from SCPs.</p>
<p>So there is absolutely a point to keeping SCPs alive if they can. There's no telling if, in a month, there's going to be a breakthrough in this technology or another that allows us to obtain more understanding of him or help alleviate his condition. Not to mention the article mentions that they have months' worth of tests lined up for him&#8212;there's no telling what kind of results those tests might produce. An SCP is almost always more useful alive than dead, unless it's actually currently threatening the fate of the world.</p>
<p>And that's why they didn't kill him as soon as he requested. Because if they had destroyed every SCP as soon as somebody felt like giving up, they wouldn't have discovered half the stuff they did.</p>

							</div>

								
					
