															</div>
				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/doctornovakaine" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(3003380); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=3003380&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1607266793" alt="DoctorNovakaine" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=3003380)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/doctornovakaine" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(3003380); return false;" >DoctorNovakaine</a></span> <span class="odate time_1532628880 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">26 Jul 2018 18:14</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-3886685">
									

<p>Apologies for the necro, but this is an issue that comes up more often than I'd like to see in SCPs, and I think it's worth continuing to bring attention to.</p>
<p>We know the lines well. &quot;The Foundation is cold, not cruel.&quot; (There's a fine line between the two, though.) &quot;It's Secure, Contain, Protect, not Destroy, Destroy, Destroy.&quot; (AKA the GOC.) But it's one thing to have the lines, another thing to make them work in practice. I think the main problem I see with some SCPs is that they take these to their conclusion without understanding why they exist.</p>
<p>And I think this is a good example. &quot;The Foundation isn't in the habit of destroying anomalies.&quot; Right, but this isn't just callous destruction, this is a question of suffering. It's very clear 2860 is suffering, to the point where he's being put in a medically-induced coma to avoid him killing himself. (So he's not going to be giving any more verbal insights into his anomaly - not that there'd be anything to give after a decade.) That would qualify under many definitions as well into the &quot;cruel&quot; arena.</p>
<p>Now, this could be justified if there was reason to believe that the Foundation was gaining key information about the anomaly and making significant progress towards something. Except&#8230;where's the products of this decade of research? What have they learned that would justify keeping him alive? They have several months of tests, WHAT ARE THEY TESTING? If the goal is insight into anomalies, what insight have they gained? And that ends up being a fundamental problem I see a lot in these &quot;we keep the anomaly alive against their will&quot; works - we rarely if ever see anything to justify that. No scientific breakthroughs, no understanding of the anomalies, no production at all, just years of torture just because. That is NOT &quot;cold, not cruel.&quot; That's blatant, bald-faced cruelty.</p>
<p>To justify avoiding the termination of an anomaly that wants to be terminated, there has to be something concrete behind it. Testing, information gain, something that justifies the actions and is demonstrably productive (not just testing for the sake of testing). Without that, the takeway becomes &quot;Foundation are monsters&quot; and I don't know what purpose that serves for anyone.</p>

							</div>

								
					
